  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "Figure 2: The figure shows the projection of prediction errors for two-dimensional states",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        197.7666473388672,
        521.89453125,
        207.39938354492188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "over a horizon of K = 2. The left figure illustrates the projection on the (R1, R2) axes (e.g.,",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        209.34173583984375,
        523.4888916015625,
        220.94833374023438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "k = 1), and the right figure displays the projection on the (R3, R4) axes (e.g., k = 2). This",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        222.8907470703125,
        521.9898071289062,
        234.49734497070312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "figure provides a comparison between the inflating hypercubes for a confidence level δ ∈(0, 1),",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        237.7481689453125,
        523.503662109375,
        248.04635620117188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "generated by the PCA approach (red hypercubes) and the method proposed in Hashemi",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        251.96263122558594,
        521.8723754882812,
        261.5953674316406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "et al. (2024b) (green hypercubes). It clearly demonstrates the superior accuracy of the PCA",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        265.51263427734375,
        522.3126220703125,
        275.1453552246094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "technique compared to the other method. The principal axes for k = 1, 2 are (r1, r2) and",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        277.0877380371094,
        521.9898071289062,
        288.6943664550781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(r3, r4), respectively.",
      "bbox": [
        88.7239990234375,
        290.6367492675781,
        186.20376586914062,
        302.2433776855469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The authors in Hashemi et al. (2024b) use the function in (5) to define the residual ρ and",
      "bbox": [
        106.93599700927734,
        315.8630676269531,
        521.9901123046875,
        325.55035400390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "compute the corresponding inflating hypercube δX. However, their technique imposes two",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        329.4120788574219,
        521.9512939453125,
        339.099365234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "conservative constraints. First, the center of the hypercube is always located at the origin.",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        343.0156555175781,
        524.0061645507812,
        352.64837646484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Second, the edges of the hypercube are restricted to be aligned with the direction of the",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        356.5656433105469,
        521.8944091796875,
        366.1983642578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "trajectory state components.",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        370.1146545410156,
        226.29830932617188,
        379.74737548828125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "To address these limitations, we propose an adjustment on the definition of the residual",
      "bbox": [
        106.93599700927734,
        383.66363525390625,
        521.8514404296875,
        393.2963562011719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ρ in equation (5), that enables us to overcome these issues. We integrate the concepts of",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        397.1580810546875,
        521.90869140625,
        406.8453674316406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Conformal Inference (CI) and Principal Component Analysis (PCA) in our new definition",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        410.76165771484375,
        521.86279296875,
        420.3943786621094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "for the residual. This approach provides the principal axes as the orientation of inflating",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        424.3116455078125,
        521.8499145507812,
        433.9443664550781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "hypercube and noticeably reduces its size. In other words, our approach enhances the",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        437.86065673828125,
        521.872314453125,
        447.4933776855469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "accuracy of conformal inference by manipulating the coordinate system, inspired by PCA.",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        451.4096374511719,
        523.9690551757812,
        461.0423583984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "However, in the context of CI, altering the coordinate system has also been addressed in",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        464.9586486816406,
        521.8833618164062,
        474.59136962890625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "other works, such as Tumu et al. (2024); Sharma et al. (2024).",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        478.50762939453125,
        386.3130187988281,
        488.1403503417969
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
